NAME
       base32 - encode data as base32 text, or decode it again

SYNOPSIS
       base32 [-w COLS] [FILE]
       base32 -d [-i] [FILE]

DESCRIPTION
       base32 writes data with only the capital letters A to Z and the digits
       2 to 7, padded with = to a multiple of eight characters. It is longer
       than base64 but safe where upper and lower case could be mixed up.
       With -d it turns such text back into the original bytes. It reads FILE,
       or standard input when no file is given, and writes to standard output.

       Spotting it: only A-Z, 2-7 and trailing = signs (base64 also has small
       letters, 0, 1, 8, 9, + and /).

       Encoding is not encryption: anyone can decode the text.

OPTIONS
       -d, --decode
              Decode instead of encode.

       -i, --ignore-garbage
              When decoding, skip characters that are not part of the
              alphabet.

       -w COLS, --wrap=COLS
              Break encoded lines after COLS characters (76 by default);
              -w 0 writes one long line.

EXAMPLES
           echo -n hello | base32
           echo NBSWY3DP | base32 -d

SEE ALSO
       base64(1), od(1), xxd(1)
